== Unreleased ==
Format: version 13, for features that change how data is decoded: the
  version 10 layout (version 12 when encrypted), and a reader refuses
  any header flag bit it does not know, where 10 to 12 ignored them.
  Only files that use such a feature are written as 13 (4.4 refuses
  them as newer); all others are still 10 or 12.
Change: -D and -G find block boundaries with the gear hash of the
  library chunker instead of a Rabin hash: 18 to 30% less CPU at -D,
  4 to 7% at -G (one thread, c7i), output within 0.5% at -D and 1.2%
  at -G. No format change: 4.4 reads the new files, and the new
  reader reads old ones.
Change: a version 12 file with bytes after its end record is refused
  as corrupt, unless they end in the seek index trailer "PCSEEK01".
Security: the version 12 file key is wiped when each job returns, not
  only when the context is destroyed, and passwords, file keys and
  chunk-object subkeys are wiped with a store the compiler cannot
  remove (memset of a buffer about to go out of scope may be dropped).
Change: a decompression that fails (a bad tag or checksum, a missing
  end record, a truncated file) removes the output file it created, as
  a cancelled one did; output to a caller's descriptor or to standard
  output, and archive extraction, are left as they are.
Security: decompressing with a password (-w, pc_set_password()) refuses
  a file that is not encrypted ("the file is not encrypted",
  PC_ERR_CRYPTO), where it decompressed it: anyone can substitute a
  plain file for an encrypted one, and nothing would authenticate it.
Fix: a data chunk whose stored length was at most 256 bytes over the
  chunk size, which the reader accepted, was read into a buffer with
  less slack than that (about 50 bytes for zstd at 64 KB chunks): a
  heap overflow found with ASan, in every format version. The read is
  now bounded by the buffer and such a length is refused as corrupt.
Security: the version 12 chunk ordinal, and the chunk counters behind
  it, are 64 bits end to end; the chunk number was 32 bits, so data
  chunk 2^32 would have reused chunk 0's key and nonce, and the end
  record's count would have wrapped. Encrypted version 11 and earlier
  files with more chunks than their 32-bit counters held are refused.
Library: chunk objects sealed with a key are object version 2 (magic
  PCK2): XChaCha20-Poly1305 with a random 192-bit nonce and the whole
  header as associated data, 16-byte tag, in place of XSalsa20 and a
  keyed BLAKE2b-256 tag. Sealing zstd:3 with a key ran at 468 MB/s and
  opening at 681 MB/s; now 897 and 2226 MB/s (bench_chunk, Xeon 8488C).
  Objects are 16 bytes smaller. Keyed version 1 objects still open;
  4.4 and earlier cannot open version 2 objects. Unkeyed objects are
  unchanged (PCK1). The dictionary flag works as before in both.
Security: encrypted .pz files are format version 12, with authenticated
  encryption. 4.4 and earlier encrypted data chunk k and metadata chunk k
  of an archive with the same key and CTR nonce (the XOR of the two
  ciphertexts is the XOR of the plaintexts; with -c none and a known
  file this decrypts the metadata), and authenticated each chunk alone,
  so chunks could be reordered or the last ones cut off (keeping the end
  marker) and the file still decompressed without an error. Version 12
  seals every chunk with an AEAD, under a nonce of its stream (data,
  metadata) and position, with its length and flags as associated data,
  and ends with an authenticated count of both kinds of chunks: a
  reordered, repeated, moved, cut or changed chunk fails. -e SALSA20 is
  now XChaCha20-Poly1305 and -e AES AES-256-GCM (OpenSSL), keyed with
  Argon2id (128 MiB, 4 passes, 4 lanes). enwik8 at zstd -l3 on a 16-vCPU
  Xeon 8488C: 0.62 s with either cipher, 0.64 s with 4.4's AES-CTR and
  HMAC, 0.34 s unencrypted; the key derivation is 0.29 s of it. Files
  written by 4.4 and earlier (version 11 and below) still decompress;
  4.4 and earlier refuse version 12 ("Cannot handle newer archive
  version 12"). Unencrypted files are unchanged (version 10).
Fix: compressing a 1- or 2-byte file with -e wrote past a heap buffer
  (the header was built in the chunk buffer, which a tiny input makes
  smaller than an encrypted header); glibc then aborted.
Library: key files (pc_keyfile_create()) use Argon2id (RFC 9106, KDF
  id 2) with 128 MiB, 4 passes and 4 lanes in place of scrypt: 0.29 s on
  a Xeon 8488C, 0.36 s on Graviton. pc_keyfile_open() still opens scrypt
  key files; 4.4 and earlier cannot open the new ones. Argon2id is the
  bundled Monocypher 4.0.2 (OpenSSL has it only from 3.2), pinned to
  the RFC 9106 vectors, and costs over 1 GiB, 16 passes, 16 lanes or
  4 GiB of memory traffic are refused before any work.
Library: pc_set_allocator() gives the library a host program's memory
  functions (malloc, realloc, free and a user pointer), process-wide,
  before its first allocation. The library's own code, the bundled zstd,
  lz4 and libbsc, and the lzma, PPMd, zlib and bzip2 allocation hooks use
  them; libarchive, OpenSSL, the C++ filters and OpenMP do not. Archive
  jobs no longer leave their temporary file names registered for
  pc_cleanup_tempfiles() after they end.
Library: pc_set_default_log(fn, arg, level) sends the messages of
  contexts without their own pc_set_log() sink, and messages logged
  outside any context, to a callback instead of stderr.
Library: pc_set_thread_hooks() runs a host program's start(name) and
  stop() hooks in every thread the library creates (workers, writer,
  archive, extract and metadata threads), so a host can register them
  with its runtime.
Library: pc_set_progress(ctx, fn, arg) reports input consumed and output
  written after each chunk of pc_compress_fd(), pc_decompress_fd() and
  start_pcompress(); a non-zero return cancels the job cleanly, with
  the new error class PC_ERR_CANCELLED.
Library: zstd dictionaries for sealed chunk objects. pc_dict_train()
  trains one from samples, pc_set_dict() makes a context seal and open
  with it, and pc_dict_id() names it. Objects sealed with a dictionary
  carry its id (flag 4 in the PCK1 header, 32 more bytes) and open only
  with that dictionary set: otherwise pc_chunk_open() fails with the new
  error class PC_ERR_DICT. On pieces under 8 KB it shrinks stored size
  by 30 to 40% (small /usr/share/doc files at zstd:3: ratio 1.89 to 2.96;
  per-directory manifests of /usr: 1.75 to 2.46); 64 KB chunks gain
  almost nothing; chunks of 128 KB and more are sealed without the
  dictionary, where it would cost up to 8%. Objects sealed without a
  dictionary are unchanged; versions before this one refuse dictionary
  objects.
Library: pc_chunk_seal() stores incompressible chunks raw without
  running the codec: sampled histograms of the bytes and of the
  differences between neighbouring bytes (2 KB, then 8 KB per 256 KB, of
  chunks from 16 KB) that are both close to uniform skip it. Sealing
  random data or video runs at 700-750 MB/s with any algorithm (zstd:9
  was 41 MB/s, lzma:6 13 MB/s); compressed .gz/.xz/.zst files lose 0.03%
  in size; text, binaries, byte ramps and tables keep their size and
  lose about 1% in seal speed. No format change.
Library: pc_chunk_id2(algorithm, ...) names chunks with BLAKE2b
  (PC_ID_BLAKE2B, as pc_chunk_id) or BLAKE3 (PC_ID_BLAKE3, keyed with a
  32-byte key): keyed BLAKE3 ids of 64 KB chunks ran at 4420 MB/s
  against 820 MB/s for BLAKE2b on one core of an EC2 Xeon 8488C. The
  official BLAKE3 C code 1.8.7 is bundled in crypto/blake3 (CC0 or
  Apache-2.0), with its run-time dispatch; tests pin the official
  vectors. Sealed objects keep their BLAKE2b tags.
Fix: a failed compress to a named output (pcompress file target, or the
  command-line style API with a target name) left the partial .pz file
  behind; it is removed now, as the temporary file is otherwise.
Fix: archive compression with -T (no metadata stream) hung for good
  when a worker or the writer failed, or when the job was cancelled:
  the archiver thread was never released.
Performance: compressing or decompressing a regular file no longer
  fills the page cache: input is read with POSIX_FADV_SEQUENTIAL and
  dropped every 32 MB, archive members as they are copied, and on Linux
  output past a tenth of RAM is handed to writeback and dropped. On a
  c7i.4xlarge (32 GB) compressing a 20 GB file with lz4 used to add
  28 GB to the page cache and leave 0 to 10 MB of a 1.9 GB file that was
  cached before; now it adds 70 MB and that file stays cached. Time is
  unchanged (288 s each way, bound by the gp3 volume). Descriptors
  passed to pc_compress_fd() and pc_decompress_fd() get no hints: how
  the caller's files stay cached is the caller's business.
Fix: a metadata chunk (archive mode) whose stored length exceeded the
  3 MB metadata buffer overflowed the heap while it was read, before any
  check (found with ASan); its original length was not bounded either.
  Both are refused now as corrupt.  So are lengths that wrapped the
  seek past a chunk back onto itself, where extraction looped for good:
  a metadata chunk's length as the main reader skips it, and a data
  chunk's length as the metadata thread skips it.
CLI: -X appends a seek index to a .pz file, and -d -r offset:length
  (single file) and -d -R member (archive) then decode only the chunks
  they need. On a c7i.4xlarge, one 20 KB member at the end of a 1.3 GB
  archive of /usr/lib comes out in 0.08 s instead of 3.0 s. The index
  costs 0.47% in size and no measurable time; -X turns off the metadata
  stream and the automatic global dedupe, and refuses -G. Readers before
  4.5 read -X files unchanged (the index follows the end-of-stream
  marker). A random read checks the index's tag and that each chunk it
  decodes is the one the index names. Encrypted (format version 12):
  the index is sealed with the file's AEAD (stream 4) and bound to the
  end record, and the chunk tags it lists are the chunks' AEAD tags.
Tests: test_alloc installs an allocator that tags every block and checks
  each free, so a block freed by the wrong allocator fails, and checks
  that no block is left once everything is freed.

== 4.4.0 Release ==
Library: pc_derive_key(master, purpose, out) derives one 256-bit key per
  named purpose from a master key, so a backup tool can name objects
  (pc_chunk_id) and seal them (pc_set_key) with different keys instead of
  the master for both. examples/backup/pcbackup.c does so; repositories
  made by older pcbackup builds do not open with this one (pcbackup is an
  example, with no stored repositories to keep).
Library: pc_chunker_new2(algorithm, ...) adds PC_CHUNKER_GEAR, FastCDC's
  gear hash with normalized chunking: 1058 MB/s against 245 MB/s for the
  Rabin chunker on one core of meh's Xeon. pc_chunker_new() is unchanged.
Library: pc_chunk_seal() and pc_chunk_open() keep their codec set up in
  the context between calls instead of making it for every chunk (zstd:3
  seals 43% faster, lzma about twice as fast), and lzma no longer sets up
  a dictionary much larger than the chunk. pc_chunk_id() called before
  any context now uses the CPU's fastest BLAKE2b code.
Library: manifests are format 2 (PCMF2): entries gain birth and change
  times, device and inode numbers, rdev, file flags, owner and group
  names, extended attributes, ACLs, a sparse mark, a subtree id for a
  manifest per directory, and hard link, device and FIFO types. Unknown
  records from a newer writer are kept and written back; critical ones
  are refused. PCMF1 manifests still decode. 4.3 and older cannot read
  PCMF2. pc_entry_t grows; programs built against the 4.3 header keep
  working through the _sz functions.
Library: pc_index_* keeps only 32 bits of hash and a record number per
  id in memory, reading the id back from the file to confirm a match:
  11 to 21 bytes per id instead of about 45. The file format is unchanged.
Tests: bench_chunk measures the chunkers, ids, seal and open ("make bench").
Windows: file names are UTF-8 everywhere, so names the code page cannot
  represent (Japanese, Greek, emoji on a Western code page) work, and so
  do paths longer than 260 characters, without LongPathsEnabled.
  pcompress.exe and the tests carry a manifest that makes UTF-8 their
  code page and declares them long-path aware (Windows 10 1903 or
  later). The library opens, creates, renames, removes, and walks files
  through the UTF-16 calls, so pc_index_open() takes a UTF-8 path even
  in a program without that manifest. All paths given to the library
  are UTF-8, on every platform.
Archives: entry names are stored as UTF-8 in the PAX headers. In the
  "C" locale every non-ASCII name used to draw "Can't translate
  pathname ... to UTF-8" and was stored as raw bytes (hdrcharset
  BINARY). Archives made on Windows now extract with the same names on
  Linux and the reverse; on Windows, names marked BINARY are read as
  UTF-8.
Archives: extracting an archive holding a sparse file failed ("Line too
  long", "Fatal error aborting extraction") unless it was made with -T.
  With the metadata stream on, sparse files are now archived as plain
  data; extraction still recreates holes.
Build: FreeBSD builds with the base clang (the x86 assembler fast paths
  are skipped where there is no GNU as). CI runs FreeBSD 14.4 and OmniOS
  (illumos x86_64) in VMs.

== 4.3.1 Release ==
Build: Alpine's GCC 15 on aarch64 crashed compiling 4.3.0 ("internal
  compiler error: in aarch64_function_arg_alignment") where an unaligned
  load was passed to musl's htonl() or ntohs(), so the 4.3.0 release has no
  arm64 static binary. The unaligned-load macros now give values of the
  plain integer type; the generated code is unchanged with GCC and clang
  on aarch64 and with GCC on SPARC. The release again has both static
  binaries.
Packaging: packaging/allowed_signers lists every signing key the
  maintainer uses, so ssh-keygen -Y verify accepts every release
  signature.

== 4.3.0 Release ==
Encryption: the key is derived with scrypt at N = 2^17 (128 MB, about
  0.3 s), not 2^11: guessing a password costs 64 times more. The header
  records the cost (format version 11), and a crafted cost is refused
  before any work. Releases before 4.3 cannot read files encrypted by
  4.3 ("Cannot handle newer archive version 11"); 4.3 reads theirs, and
  unencrypted files are still version 10, readable by both.
Archives: on Linux, file types were not recognised from their contents
  (ELF and PE executables, BMP, COFF, DOS COM, TZif, PPMd, WavPack), only
  from their names, since 2013: pc_archive.c asks for strict X/Open, which
  hides glibc's BYTE_ORDER, and the code then took the big-endian magic
  numbers. The byte order now comes from the compiler (__BYTE_ORDER__)
  everywhere, so -x and the type-based choices of adapt and adapt2 apply
  to such files as intended; archives may differ from 4.2.1's, and both
  extract with either release. <arpa/nameser_compat.h> is no longer used.
Fixes: when a worker thread failed to start (setup error, no memory),
  the workers already started were neither stopped nor joined, and their
  memory was freed under them. Decompressing a file made with global
  dedupe left one descriptor open per thread, and each archive
  extraction leaked a pipe: a long-lived program using the library ran
  out of descriptors. An empty input file was not closed.
Windows: pcompress builds on Windows 11, x86_64 and ARM64, with MSYS2 and
  the MinGW-w64 clang toolchain: pcompress.exe and libpcompress-1.dll (the
  public API only), tested in CI. File I/O is binary throughout, archive
  mode walks directories, the password prompt does not echo. Archives do
  not keep symbolic links, owners or POSIX permissions there. No MSVC
  build yet.
Packaging: releases add .rpm (Fedora) and .deb (Debian) packages for x86_64
  and arm64 from packaging/, a static (musl) binary for both, and a signed
  build-provenance attestation for every file (gh attestation verify).
  ./config --disable-rpath and make install LIBDIR=... for distribution
  builds; make static. packaging/README.md lists what a release ships.
Library: pc_entry_t can grow. pc_manifest_add/get/find now pass the
  caller's sizeof (pc_entry_t) to new pc_manifest_*_sz functions, so
  programs built against a later or earlier header keep working; the
  old functions remain for programs already built. ABI unchanged.
Build fixes found by the static build: our copies of OpenSSL's AES
  assembler are renamed (pc_aesni_*, pc_vpaes_*) so they no longer clash
  with libcrypto's in a static link; ./config probes GCC's Graphite loop
  flags on a real loop nest (Alpine's GCC 15 crashes in them); packPNM
  includes <stdint.h> (musl).
Code: start_decompress_impl() (1,069 lines) is split into a 191-line
  coordinator and 18 steps; no behaviour change.
Testing: fuzz targets for pc_chunk_open(), pc_keyfile_open() and
  pc_manifest_decode(), with seeds, run nightly for 10 minutes each under
  ASan. CI adds LTO builds (gcc, clang), the packages, Windows, and a
  nightly coverage report (79% of lines, 91% of functions).

== 4.2.1 Release ==
Data corruption: archives made with -x, or at -l9 and above (which turn
  -x on), could hold a chunk that does not decode, depending on the bytes
  of executables in them. Compression reported success; extraction stops
  with "Chunk N, checksums do not match". The E8E9 filter's encoder could
  change bytes its decoder does not restore. It no longer does, and the
  decoder is unchanged, so archives written now also extract with older
  releases. Archives already affected stay unreadable in that chunk; the
  checksum reports it. This was the intermittent archive failure in CI.
libbsc: built with LIBBSC_NO_UNALIGNED_ACCESS on every host. GCC 16 at
  -O3 vectorised libbsc's LZP match loop into aligned loads on unaligned
  data, and -c libbsc at -l1 or -l3 (and adapt2, which may pick it)
  crashed on some text. Speed is unchanged.
Large files: the build passed -D_FILE_OFFSET_BITS=64 misspelled, so 32-bit
  builds had a 32-bit off_t; a build now fails if off_t is not 64 bits.
Signals: an interrupted semaphore wait is retried instead of returning
  early, which could end an archive extraction as if the stream were done.
Hang: decompressing a damaged file could hang instead of failing (about
  1 in 25 runs on 4 CPUs). A worker left its loop when any chunk had
  failed, stranding the chunk it had just been handed.
Archive extraction names the real error when a chunk fails, instead of
  only "Truncated tar archive", and no longer can leave the writer
  waiting after the extractor closes.
Library: pc_set_algorithm() copies the name; it kept the caller's pointer.
Build: the bundled libarchive is configured without optional libraries
  (zstd, lz4, libb2, lzo2, mbedtls, nettle, libmd, cng), so it never links
  a system copy of them; CI checks this.
Dependencies: libbsc 3.4.0 (fixes data corruption in libbsc's multithreaded
  compression, present since 3.1.0; not reproduced through pcompress, whose
  output is unchanged), lz4 and WavPack at upstream's newest commits.
  libarchive (3.8.10) and zstd were already current.
Big-endian hosts: ./config builds without libbsc (it is little-endian only
  and 3.4 does not compile there); pcompress already refused it at run time.
Release gate: ci/gate.sh, ci/remote-gate.sh, ci/xcompat.sh and
  ci/pwcompat.sh (docs/PLATFORMS.md). Unit tests in CI run under a
  watchdog that prints backtraces if one hangs; CI jobs time out at 60
  minutes.
Nightly CI builds with --portable so valgrind does not meet AVX-512.

== 4.2.0 Release ==
Library: chunk-level building blocks for backup tools (libpcompress(3),
  "Chunks"): a streaming content-defined chunker (pc_chunker_*), keyed
  BLAKE2b-256 chunk ids (pc_chunk_id), and sealed chunk objects
  (pc_chunk_seal/pc_chunk_open: compressed, optionally XSalsa20-encrypted
  and BLAKE2b-authenticated, self-describing; pc_set_key takes a raw
  256-bit key). examples/pzstore.c is a small deduplicating store on them.
  Key files (pc_keyfile_create/open): a random key wrapped with a password
  of any length (scrypt).
  Chunk index (pc_index_*): a persistent, append-only set of chunk ids;
  examples/pzstore uses it.
  Manifests (pc_manifest_*): a canonical encoding of one snapshot's
  entries and their chunk ids; the decoder treats its input as hostile.
  examples/backup/pcbackup.c: a snapshot backup tool on the public API
  (backup, list, restore all or one file, forget, prune), run by make check.
  Additions only: the 4.1.0 ABI is unchanged.
  (Sealed objects written by development snapshots before this fix do not
  open: their subkeys could be learned from a chunk id; see git log.)
Passwords: the whole password is used, up to 4096 bytes. 4.1.0 used only
  the first 15 bytes of a -w file over 16 bytes, and its prompt refused
  passwords over 14 bytes. Files written then still decrypt (the old form
  is tried too); files encrypted now with passwords over 16 bytes cannot be
  read by 4.1.0 or earlier.
Security: pc_chunk_open() no longer hands crafted chunk headers to
  decompressors that overflow (lz4 level 2, lzma, ppmd level 15, adapt);
  found by review and a new mutation fuzz test, none reachable from .pz.
Dependencies: libarchive 3.8.10 (from 3.7.9: thirteen releases, mostly
  security fixes), zstd, lz4, libbsc and WavPack at upstream's newest
  commits; flake.lock refreshed. Release tarballs ship libarchive's
  generated configure, so building one needs no autotools (RHEL 9's
  autoconf is too old for libarchive 3.8).
make check also runs the backward-compatibility suite and the example
  backup tool. CI adds Fedora and Rocky Linux 9 (x86_64, arm64).
docs/PLATFORMS.md: the supported-platform matrix and what gates a release.

== 4.1.0 Release ==
Library:
libpcompress is now a library other programs can use: an installed header
  <pcompress/pcompress.h>, pkg-config file and libpcompress(3) man page.
  New API: pc_new/pc_free, typed setters (pc_set_algorithm, _level,
  _chunk_size, _threads, _checksum, _dedupe, _encryption, _password),
  pc_compress_fd/pc_decompress_fd, pc_set_log, pc_last_error,
  pc_last_error_code (stable pc_error_t classes), pc_strerror,
  pc_cleanup_tempfiles, pc_version. The command-line style API is kept.
Several contexts can run at once in one process (they used to crash).
Only the public API is exported; the bundled zstd, lz4, libbsc and
  libarchive symbols are hidden, so programs can link their own copies.
The library no longer installs signal handlers, calls exit() or chdir().
The slab allocator was removed (process-global, and no faster than
  malloc); -M, ALLOCATOR_BYPASS and --disable-allocator are ignored.

Fixes:
A corrupt file could, about 1 time in 200, decompress with exit status 0
  and truncated output.
-j and -x (packJPG/packPNM, Dispack) were ignored; packJPG also did
  nothing on arm64, where char is unsigned.
A crafted archive with absolute member paths could write outside the
  extraction directory.
./config failed on illumos/Solaris in 4.0.x (OpenMP probe under ksh93).
Bundled libarchive: tar entry numbering used function statics (a race
  between concurrent extractions); now per archive, as upstream.
LZMA SDK: three match-finder fields were read before being set
  (valgrind).

Compatibility: the file format is unchanged (version 10). 4.0.2 and 4.1.0
  read each other's files, archives and streams in both directions, and
  the 4.0.2 binary runs on the 4.1.0 libpcompress.so.1.

== 4.0.2 Release ==
'make -j check' no longer relinks libpcompress while the tests run it
  (archive tests failed under parallel make, e.g. in nixpkgs' ofborg).
zstd no longer prints "Destination buffer is too small" for every
  incompressible chunk (output was always correct).

== 4.0.1 Release ==
Binary tarballs run from wherever they are unpacked: the binary finds
  libpcompress through $ORIGIN/../lib (@loader_path on macOS), so no
  LD_LIBRARY_PATH is needed. The release workflow checks this.
Codeberg releases are published by CI alongside GitHub's.

== 4.0.0 Release ==
Compatibility: the file format version is unchanged (10, as in 3.1).
Verified: files from the earlier 4.0 development builds and the
test/backward_compat reference archives decompress byte-identically.

Correctness and security:
Every ARM64 BLAKE2b digest was wrong (NEON); BLAKE2bp was wrong with few
  OpenMP threads; SHA256/SHA512 now give the same digest on every CPU.
Big-endian (SPARC) support: DICT, LZP, CRC, metadata and archive fixes;
  SALSA20 files are now portable between byte orders; libbsc is refused
  on big-endian (upstream does not round-trip there).
Strict-alignment CPUs: no more SIGBUS (dedupe, libbsc, lzp, LZMA, xxhash,
  dispack).
Hardened every decoder against corrupt or truncated input: no hangs
  (zlib, bzip2, PPMd, worker/writer shutdown, archive extraction), crashes
  or overruns (LZP, DICT, lzfx, AES-CTR, chunk/dedupe/encryption headers).
Fixed a stack overflow when reading a 16-byte password file, archive
  extraction that silently produced nothing on illumos, an archive
  extraction hang (~9% of runs), -K extraction desync, 1-byte chunks,
  '-d foo.pz' writing foo.pz.out, and exit status 0 on write failures.
Memory: fixed leaks and allocator mismatches found by ASan/LSan/UBSan;
  64-byte-aligned BLAKE2/Keccak HMAC state (clang/AVX crash).

Usability:
Usage errors exit 2, -h/-V exit 0; unknown -c/-e names are rejected.
New pcompress(1) man page and DESIGN.md; installed binary finds its
  library via rpath.

Build and portability:
Builds and passes its tests on Linux x86_64/arm64/riscv64, illumos SPARC,
  macOS arm64/x86_64 with GCC and Clang (incl. LTO); config options
  --portable, --enable-lto, --enable-coverage; OpenMP probing (Apple
  clang + libomp); non-executable stack; MSVC can build the core
  compressors. The unbuildable CMake build was removed.

Testing:
Unit tests (KATs for every hash, crypto, filters, every compressor's
  corrupt-input paths), integration and error-path suites, a feature
  matrix of ~170 option combinations, CI on Codeberg and GitHub
  (incl. ASan/UBSan and the Nix package), nightly full matrix/Valgrind.

== 3.1 Bugfix Release ==
Avoid auto-selection variable chunking for buffer sizes below threshold.
Do not auto-select Global Dedupe for below threshold buffers.
Fix issue #18.
Do not try to generate a target filename in pipe mode.
Scale default compression buffer size for levels > 8.
Fix issue #17.
Use LZ4 and Libbsc extra padding space for the compression buffer in adaptive modes.
Fix issue #16.
Fix issue #15.
Fix issue #14
Updated fix for issue #12
Add check for correct Libarchive version.
Update header checks to abort if not found.

== 3.0 Major Beta Release ==
Basic capability to list contents of an archive without extracting to disk.
Bump version and update command help text.
Update README with additional option details.
Change nftw() to depth-first scan to handle restoring directory permissions correctly.
When sorting cause directories to be sorted after files and in descending order of nesting level.
Take out stray printf().
Detect some DICOM formats and use BSC for DICOM data.
Overhaul documentation part #1
Detect and handle uncompressed PDF files using libbsc.
